  "query_preview": "..\n\nGusty southeast winds that had preceded the approaching upper \nridge have diminished across the area allowing the Lake Wind \nAdvisory that had been in effect across Fort Peck Lake to expire \non time. Next, attention focuses on seasonably warm and dry \nweather as the ridge translates east through the midweek across NE\nMontana. High temperatures will peak on Thursday as the ridge \naxis begins to push downstream of the CWA.\n\nMeanwhile, a deepening upper level trough will send southerly\nupper level flow across the region as a surface cold front slides\nthrough Thursday night and Friday. As Pacific moisture advances\nahead of this trough, rain chances will increase behind the front\nThursday night into Friday before shifting east by Saturday\nmorning. Current ensemble means depict a quarter to a half of an\ninch over the CWA with lesser amounts toward Wibaux.\n\nLook for drier albeit far cooler weather conditions for the \nweekend behind the departing system.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNBM held the line this forecast cycle capturing large scale \ntrends in the short to medium term efficiently given the benign \nweather expected with the upper level ridge. Confidence continues \nto increase on precipitation chances by Friday, though details on \nprecipitation timing and placement as well as specific amounts \nwill come into focus as the event heads into the short term and is\nbetter resolved by high resolution models.",
